# Day 38 - Maximum Ascending Subarray Sum

## Problem Statement
Given an array of positive integers `nums`, return the maximum possible sum of a **strictly increasing** subarray.

A subarray is a contiguous sequence of elements within the array.

## Code

class Solution {
public:
    int maxAscendingSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int currSum = nums[0], maxSum = nums[0];
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i - 1]) {
                currSum += nums[i];
            }
            else {
                currSum = nums[i];
            }

            maxSum = max(currSum, maxSum);
        }

        return maxSum;
    }
};




## Time Complexity
**O(n)**

## Space Complexity
**O(1)**

## Approach
This solution traverses the array while maintaining the sum of the current **strictly increasing** subarray. If the current element is greater than the previous element, it extends the current increasing subarray, and its value is added to `currSum`. Otherwise, the increasing sequence is broken, so `currSum` is reset to the current element. After processing each element, `maxSum` is updated to store the maximum ascending subarray sum found so far. Since the array is traversed only once and only a few variables are used, the algorithm runs in linear time with constant extra space.
